Every package has two independent classifications: where it comes from and how the compiler obtains its implementation.

### Package origin

Origin describes ownership and distribution:

- **Core**: compiler-owned packages such as `@core/html`, `@core/css`, `@core/io` and `@core/math`.
- **Builder**: packages selected by a project builder, including `@builder/web/site` and `@builder/web/canvas`.
- **ProjectLocal**: scoped `@package.moth` support packages, the project-root package facade and annotated project-local JavaScript files.
- **Dependency**: reserved for future package-system support.

### Package backing

Backing describes implementation:

- **MothSource**: one or more Moth modules resolved through the source package registry.
- **ExternalBinding**: typed functions, constants and opaque types implemented outside Moth.

Backing is semantic, not a file-format choice. Precompiling or emitting a `MothSource` package as Wasm keeps it `MothSource`. Imported WIT components are `ExternalBinding` packages.

[table:
    [th:
        [: **Package**]
        [: **Origin**]
        [: **Backing**]
    ]

    [td: [:`@core/html`, `@core/css`] [: Core] [: MothSource]]
    [td: [:`@builder/web/site`] [: Builder] [: MothSource]]
    [td: [:`@core/collections`, `@core/io`, `@core/math`, `@core/text`, `@core/random`, `@core/time`] [: Core] [: ExternalBinding]]
    [td: [:`@builder/web/canvas`, `@builder/web/graphics`, `@builder/web/page`] [: Builder] [: ExternalBinding]]
    [td: [: Scoped `@package.moth` support package] [: ProjectLocal] [: MothSource]]
    [td: [: Project-root `@package.moth` facade] [: ProjectLocal] [: MothSource]]
    [td: [: Annotated project-local `.js` file] [: ProjectLocal] [: ExternalBinding]]
]

Origin and backing don't change dependency clause syntax or module visibility. Source-backed packages publish their public surface with `$export`. Binding-backed packages expose typed external symbols registered by the builder.

### Package names and source facades

Core and Builder names sit in reserved namespaces. Core registrations live below `@core/`. Builder registrations live below `@builder/` at an explicit path the builder chooses, such as `web/site` or `game`. The exact `@core` and `@builder` roots are not importable package surfaces. ProjectLocal and Dependency packages cannot claim either root. Registration compares names case-insensitively, so case-only variants fail along with equal or ancestor/descendant overlaps for every backing.

Every registered MothSource package publishes its public surface through a directly contained `@package.moth`. `@mod.moth` alone is insufficient. A co-located `@mod.moth` may still own a separate internal normal-position scope, while the external package interface always comes from `@package.moth`. Ordinary project modules keep their optional root combinations. Binding-backed packages carry no source facade because the builder registers their typed symbols directly.

Copying a package directory into a project does not grant Core or Builder identity. A copied source package becomes an ordinary ProjectLocal support package through its own `@package.moth`. A copied binding package directory registers no symbols and gains no namespace identity.

### Prelude is visibility policy

The prelude is implicit dependency policy, not an origin or backing. The ordinary Moth prelude exposes `io` as an alias to `@core/io`. The separate `.mtf` prelude makes public compile-time constants from `@core/html` and `@core/css` visible by bare name. Package metadata remains independent of these visibility policies.

### Builder-selected availability

Builders provide implementations and additional packages without redefining
Core semantics. Official browser and native environments provide the canonical
Core catalogue, including its documented host-capability gates. Custom builders
may provide a subset or no public Core implementation. Reserved Core identities
and the `io` prelude keep their meaning even when an operation is unavailable.
The progress matrix records current implementation gaps separately from this
design contract.

The mandatory Core source packages `@core/html` and `@core/css` are an exception to
builder-selected availability. The common builder surface registers both roots
under every builder, including custom SDK builders, because they supply
compiler-level template helpers rather than host capabilities. A builder cannot
replace either root or register a package under its prefix.

The future official package manager classifies managed Moth-source packages as
no-core or Core-dependent over their complete dependency closure. Official
certified environments may consume both. Custom builders receive no-core
packages only, even when they use Lappet or implement all Core APIs. Certification
is a Moth project decision, not inferred from matching signatures.

Compiler-internal helpers and the unused `io` prelude do not make a package
Core-dependent. No-core still requires support for the language features it
executes. Managed Builder-dependent and foreign-code packages are deferred.
Local and builder-supplied packages retain their normal compiler rules. See
@../lappet (Lappet) for scope and the package-manager design.

### Binding-backed package constraints

Binding-backed packages expose free functions, constants and opaque types. They don't expose source-style receiver methods. Use a source-backed wrapper type for method-style APIs. External calls are positional-only because named arguments and default parameters are source-function features.

The HTML-Wasm mode has a stricter backend surface. A reachable JS-backed package needs a Wasm lowering before that backend can use it.

### No package manager yet

There is no remote fetching, lockfile or version resolution yet. Use `@package.moth` support facades for project-local source-backed packages, builder-provided package dependency clauses for built-in capabilities and annotated `.js` wrappers for direct browser integration.

### Concepts related to package origins and backing

- For project-local packages see @#project-local-packages (Project-local packages).
- For dependency path families see @#dependency-paths (Dependency paths).
- For Core package API details see @./core (Core packages).
